import EnglishHeader from '../../../components/EnglishHeader.astro'; import EnglishFooter from '../../../components/EnglishFooter.astro'; const base=import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/,''); const images=`${base}/images/bikes/giame`;
---
<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="A rare early-1950s Giamé racer from Turin with Simplex Tour de France and drilled Giamé parts."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/bikes/giame/hero.webp"/><title>Giamé · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="bikes" danishPath="/cykler/giame/"/><main class="atala-page"><header class="quick-hero"><p class="section-label">In the collection · Turin · early 1950s</p><h1>Giamé</h1><p>A rare Turin racer whose drilled seatpost, French derailleur and surviving maker parts show the pursuit of low weight.</p></header><figure class="quick-pass"><img src={`${images}/hero.webp`} alt="Early-1950s Giamé racing bicycle"/><figcaption>From an older Turin collector. Photo: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure><section class="atala-story"><div><h2>The Giamé brothers</h2></div><div><p>Antonio and Agostino Giamé belonged to Turin’s close framebuilding world. Collector accounts connect them with Frejus and Gios, but the sequence is not securely documented.</p><p>The former owner dated this among the earliest known Giamé bicycles. That is provenance, not a production record.</p></div></section><section class="atala-details"><figure><img src={`${images}/seatpost.webp`} alt="Drilled Giamé seatpost"/><figcaption>Weight saving made visible.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/cockpit.webp`} alt="Cockpit and aluminium bottles"/><figcaption>Cloth tape and cork-stoppered bottles.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/drivetrain.webp`} alt="Giamé drivetrain"/><figcaption>Simplex gearing on an Italian bicycle.</figcaption></figure></section><section class="atala-components"><div><h2>Italian bicycle, French gears</h2></div><div><p>The Simplex Tour de France derailleur required feel at the lever. Earlier records name FB hubs, Nisi rims and Universal 51 brakes, with saddle, bars and seatpost attributed to Giamé.</p><p>Stories say Fausto Coppi used seatposts from the workshop. No order, photograph or catalogue confirms this here; it does not make this Coppi’s bicycle.</p></div></section><section class="atala-closing"><p>The parts and Turin provenance preserve a rare trace of a workshop otherwise nearly lost.</p><div class="bike-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>← Back</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1950-1959/`}>View the period →</a></div></section></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
